##### help create
create — make a database or a bucket
====================================

Create a database:
  create database <name> [{ <options> }]

  create database app                            One copy (this node's default).
  create database app { replication_factor: 1 }  Say how many copies.

  Options: { replication_factor: <n>, storage: disk }. More than one copy
  needs that many data nodes: a factor the cluster cannot hold is refused
  before anything is written. Every database is kept on disk in this build.

Create a bucket:
  create bucket <name> [in <db>] { <Field> <type>, … }

  create bucket User { Name string, Purchases fifoStore }
  create bucket Order in shop { Total double, Lines fifoStore, Notes hashStore }

  Declare every field. A field is a scalar (string, int, double, bool,
  datetime, …) or a store, and a store belongs to each document: every User
  has its own Purchases queue. A store keeps a sum or a count current when
  you add with (sum Total, count by Status) after it. Not built yet, so
  refused: index <IndexName> ( <Field> [desc] ). All the words: help types.
  Like SQL CREATE TABLE, except a field can be a store each row owns.

Also: help create user.

Next: use app, then db.User.insert({_id_: "ada", Name: "Ada"})

##### help alter
alter — change a bucket or a user
=================================

  alter bucket <name> [in <db>] { add <Field> <type>, … }

  alter bucket User { add Email string, drop Age }
  alter bucket User in app { add Notes hashStore }

  Add fields to a bucket or drop them, in one statement: { add Email string,
  drop Age }. Adding rewrites no document: every existing document reads the
  new field as unset, and a store field is empty until its first push.
  Dropping a field makes it unreadable at once and its values are deleted in
  the background; the name can be added again later as a new field. A
  field's type never changes: add a new field, copy the values, drop the old
  one. Not built yet, so refused: add index <IndexName> ( <Field> [desc], …
  ) and drop index <IndexName>.
  Like SQL ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN and DROP COLUMN, except an add costs
  nothing however many rows there are.

Also: help alter database, help alter user, help alter user roles.

Next: describe bucket User, to see the fields now in force

##### help drop
drop — delete a bucket or a user
================================

  drop bucket <name> [in <db>]

  drop bucket User
  drop bucket User in app

  Drop a bucket and every document and store it holds. The bucket stops
  answering at once and the statement returns; its documents are deleted in
  the background, 'show buckets' lists it as dropping until they are gone,
  and the name is free to create again then (the new bucket is empty). There
  is no prompt and no undo: run 'describe bucket' first if you are not sure
  which bucket this is. Repeating a drop of a dropping bucket is safe, but a
  drop repeated after it finished and the name was created again drops the
  new bucket.
  Like SQL DROP TABLE.

Also: help drop database, help drop user.

Next: show buckets, to watch it leave the list

##### help show
show — list what exists
=======================

The databases you can use:
  show databases [limit <n>] [cursor <hex>]

  show databases
  show databases limit 10

  List the databases you hold a role on with their id and replication
  factor, a page at a time; a page that is not the last prints the statement
  that fetches the next.

A database's buckets:
  show buckets [in <db>] [limit <n>] [cursor <hex>]

  show buckets in app
  show buckets
  show buckets in app limit 10

  List a database's buckets, a page at a time; a page that is not the last
  prints the statement that fetches the next.

Draw the node summary again:
  show banner

  show banner

  Draw the box of facts about the node you are connected to: its id,
  version, uptime, cluster, replication factor and machine.

Also: help show users, help show placement, help show replication.

Next: use app

##### help describe
describe — look at a database or a bucket
=========================================

On a bucket (its documents):
  describe database <name> [limit <n>] [cursor <hex>]

  describe database app
  describe database app limit 10
  describe bucket <name> [in <db>]

  describe bucket User in app
  describe bucket User

On a store field (one document's elements):
  db.<bucket>["<id>"].<storeField>.describe()

  db.User["ada"].Recipes.describe()

Works on fifoStore, lifoStore, heapStore, hashStore and btreeStore.
Next: help sum reads one.

##### help db
db — documents and their stores
===============================

Every document statement starts with db.<bucket> and runs in the database
'use' selected. A document's id is its _id_ field.

One document:
  db.<bucket>["<id>"].get([{…}])                One document by id.
  db.<bucket>.insert({…})                       Add a document.
  db.<bucket>.upsert({…})                       Add, or replace the same _id_.
  db.<bucket>.update({…})                       Change some of its fields.
  db.<bucket>.delete({…})                       Remove it and its stores.
Many documents:
  db.<bucket>.list([{…}])                       Documents, a page at a time.
  db.<bucket>.count([{…}])                      How many documents.
A document's own stores:
  db.<bucket>["<id>"].<storeField>.<verb>([…])  push, pop, peek, claim, put, …

  db.User.insert({_id_: "ada", Name: "Ada"})
  db.User["ada"].get()
  db.User.list({filter: {Name: "Ada"}, limit: 10})
  db.User["ada"].Purchases.push({Item: "coffee"})

Coming from a document store: find({_id_: "ada"}) is db.User["ada"].get();
find({…}) is db.User.list({filter: {…}}). A store field is written through
its own verb.
Next: help insert, help list, help push.

##### help get
get — read one
==============

On a bucket (its documents):
  db.<bucket>["<id>"].get([{…}])

  db.User["ada"].get()
  db.User["ada"].get({fields: ["Name"]})

On a store field (one document's elements):
  db.<bucket>["<id>"].<storeField>.get("<key>")

  db.User["ada"].Friends.get("grace")

On a fifoStore or lifoStore get takes the lease id claim printed and prints
the elements that lease still holds:
db.User["ada"].Purchases.get("9f2c4e1ab7d34c1e8a0b5d6c7e8f9012").
Works on fifoStore, lifoStore, hashStore, btreeStore and heapStore.
Next: help take removes it instead; help claim makes a lease.

##### help list
list — read many, a page at a time
==================================

On a bucket (its documents):
  db.<bucket>.list([{…}])

  db.User.list()
  db.User.list({limit: 10})
  db.User.list({filter: {Name: "Ada"}, limit: 10})
  db.User.list({fields: ["Name", "Age"], limit: 10})

On a store field (one document's elements):
  db.<bucket>["<id>"].<storeField>.list([{limit: <n>, cursor: "<hex>"}])

  db.User["ada"].Purchases.list({limit: 10})

A page that is not the last ends with a 'more:' line: the same statement
with the cursor filled in. Run it for the next page.
Works on fifoStore, lifoStore, heapStore, hashStore and btreeStore.
Next: help count.

##### help count
count — how many
================

On a bucket (its documents):
  db.<bucket>.count([{…}])

  db.User.count()
  db.User.count({filter: {Name: "Ada"}})

On a store field (one document's elements):
  db.<bucket>["<id>"].<storeField>.count([{by: "<Field>"}])

  db.User["ada"].Purchases.count()

On a bucket with 'expire at', count reads one extra key per document, and on
a store that expires its elements it walks them, because it must skip what
has expired (help expire).
Works on fifoStore, lifoStore, heapStore, hashStore, btreeStore and
blobStore.
Next: help list.

##### help insert
insert — add a document
=======================

  db.<bucket>.insert({…})

  db.User.insert({_id_: "ada", Name: "Ada"})

  Add a document; an existing _id_ is refused. A store field is written
  through its own verb, never here.

Next: db.User["ada"].get()

##### help upsert
upsert — add, or replace the same _id_
======================================

  db.<bucket>.upsert({…})

  db.User.upsert({_id_: "ada", Name: "Ada Lovelace"})

  Add a document or replace the one with the same _id_.

Next: db.User["ada"].get()

##### help update
update — change some of its fields
==================================

  db.<bucket>.update({…})

  db.User.update({_id_: "ada", $set: {Name: "Ada King"}})
  db.User.update({_id_: "ada", $set: {Name: "Ada King"}, $inc: {Age: 1}})
  db.User.update({_id_: "ada", if: {Name: "Ada"}, $set: {Name: "Ada King"}, return: "after"})
  db.User.update({_id_: "ada", $inc: {Age: 1}, return: "before"})

  Change fields of the document with this _id_, with update operators ($set,
  $inc and the rest). All of them apply together or none does. Add if:
  {Field: value} to change it only while those scalar fields still hold
  those values (null matches a field that was never set); if one differs
  nothing changes and you get the current document. Add return: "after" or
  "before" to get the document back. An upsert takes no if.

Next: db.User["ada"].get()

##### help delete
delete — remove it and its stores
=================================

  db.<bucket>.delete({…})

  db.User.delete({_id_: "ada"})
  db.User.delete({_id_: "ada", if: {Name: "Ada"}})

  Delete the document with this _id_ and the stores it owns. Add if: {Field:
  value} to delete it only while those scalar fields still hold those
  values, and return: "before" to get the document as it was.

Next: db.User.count()

##### help grant
grant — add one grant
=====================

  grant role <role>@<db> to <user>

  grant role db-manager@app to ada
  grant role reader@* to ada

  Add one grant. Roles: root, reader, writer, db-manager, user-manager,
  cluster-manager, backup-operator, restore-operator.

##### help revoke
revoke — remove one grant
=========================

  revoke role <role>@<db> from <user>

  revoke role writer@app from ada

  Remove one grant.

##### help user
user — look at users
====================

List the users:
  user list

  user list

  The same as show users.

Look at one user:
  user describe <name>

  user describe ada

  One user's roles and status.

##### help join
join — register a running node
==============================

  join <node>@<host:port>

  join node-b@127.0.0.1:4443

  Register a running node as a member; it seeds, then becomes active.

##### help promote
promote — make a member a voter
===============================

  promote <node>

  promote node-b

  Make a member a voter, if the cluster has a free voter slot.

##### help replace-node
replace-node — reseed a node that lost data
===========================================

  replace-node <node> --with <node>@<host:port> [--accept-loss]

  replace-node node-b --with node-b@127.0.0.1:4443
  replace-node node-b --with node-b@127.0.0.1:4443 --accept-loss

  Bring back a member that lost its data: start it again with its node id,
  keys and address and an empty ring directory, wait until 'nodes' shows it
  down, then run this on any member. It pulls every pair it owns from the
  other copies and becomes active when it holds them. The node and its
  address are its own: --with names the same node. Refused when the node
  held the only copy of some pairs; --accept-loss replaces it anyway, and
  those documents are lost.

##### help nodes
nodes — list the members
========================

  nodes

  nodes

  List the members with their states and addresses, the voter count and the
  epoch. Which members are witnesses is not shown yet.

##### help status
status — slot pulls in progress
===============================

List the slots this node is copying:
  status seeding [<db> [<slot>]]

  status seeding
  status seeding app
  status seeding app 5

  List every slot this node is copying or keeping: placing (queued),
  seeding, stalled (no chunk applied for a while) and retired (the slot left
  this node's copy set and its range waits for its deletion gate), with the
  node it reads from, how much is copied, how many entries and how long ago
  the last chunk arrived. A stalled or refused row says why and names the
  statement that fixes it. Slots that are ready and serving are not listed.
  Add a database, and a slot, to narrow it; the slot is decimal or 0x hex.
  It lists up to 2000 rows. Needs the cluster-manager role.

Epoch, leader and lease:
  status

  status

  Show this node's view: its epoch, the leader it knows, whether it is
  serving, and the voters.

Next: seeding retry <db> <slot>, for a stalled row

##### help seeding
seeding — restart or forget a slot's pull
=========================================

Start a slot's pull again:
  seeding retry <db> <slot>

  seeding retry app 5
  seeding retry app 0x0a4e

  Start the pull of one slot again from a fresh cut, on another legal source
  if there is one. Use it on a row 'status seeding' shows as stalled. The
  statement returns at once; 'status seeding' shows the new attempt. It is
  refused, changing nothing, for a slot this node has no seeding record for.
  Needs the cluster-manager role.

Forget a retired slot:
  seeding forget <db> <slot> [anyway]

  seeding forget app 5
  seeding forget app 5 anyway

  Remove the record of a retired slot whose deletion gate waits for a copy
  that will never come. Its data stays on disk until another copy accepts
  this node's next pull of the slot or this node leaves the slot's copy set
  again; either deletes it. Refused, changing nothing, for a slot that is
  not retired (SEED_HELD), with no record (SEED_NOT_HELD), or retired with
  no other member holding a copy (SEED_HELD): this node may hold the only
  copy, so add anyway to forget it. Needs the cluster-manager role.

Next: status seeding <db> <slot>, to watch it

##### help use
use — work inside a database
============================

  use <database>

  use app

  Select a database, so 'in <db>' can be left out.

Next: show buckets

##### help set
set — change how results print
==============================

  set <setting> [on|off]

  set pretty on
  set extjson
  set pager off

  Change how output is printed: pretty (several lines per document), extjson
  (dates as {"$date": ...}) or pager (a screen at a time). 'set pretty' on
  its own says what it is.

##### help help
help — find a statement
=======================

  help [<topic>]

  help
  help create bucket

  List the statements, or show one with its examples: help create, help db,
  help push.

##### help push
push — add an element to a document's queue or stack
====================================================

  db.<bucket>["<id>"].<storeField>.push({…})
      Add one element to a fifoStore (a queue) or a lifoStore (a stack).

  [h]oardDB [app]> db.User["ada"].Purchases.push({Item: "coffee"})
  OK  pushed: Purchases now holds 1 element

  The document must exist first: db.User.insert({_id_: "ada", Name: "Ada"}).
  Works on fifoStore and lifoStore. For a hashStore or btreeStore use put
  (help put). push on a heapStore is not available yet.

Next: db.User["ada"].Purchases.pop() takes it off again (help pop).
Every store and its verbs: help types.

##### help pop
pop — remove and return the next elements
=========================================

  db.<bucket>["<id>"].<storeField>.pop([<n>])
      Take the next element out of a fifoStore (the oldest), a lifoStore
      (the newest) or a heapStore and print it, or the next n (1 to 100) in
      one write.

  [h]oardDB [app]> db.User["ada"].Purchases.push({Item: "coffee"})
  [h]oardDB [app]> db.User["ada"].Purchases.pop()
  {"Item": "coffee"}

  An empty store answers NOT_FOUND and names the statement that adds to it.
  A pop of several must fit about 1 KiB together, or it is refused with the
  count that fits; claim and ack have no such limit. Works on fifoStore,
  lifoStore and heapStore.

Next: db.User["ada"].Purchases.count() says how many are left (help count).
Every store and its verbs: help types.

##### help peek
peek — read the next elements without removing them
===================================================

  db.<bucket>["<id>"].<storeField>.peek([<n>])
      Print the next element, or the next n (1 to 100), and leave them in
      the store. A heapStore's lines start with the entry id and the
      priority.

  [h]oardDB [app]> db.User["ada"].Purchases.push({Item: "coffee"})
  [h]oardDB [app]> db.User["ada"].Purchases.peek()
  {"Item": "coffee"}

  Works on fifoStore, lifoStore and heapStore.

Next: db.User["ada"].Purchases.pop() takes the next one off (help pop).
Every store and its verbs: help types.

##### help sum
sum — read a sum the store keeps current
========================================

  db.<bucket>["<id>"].<storeField>.sum("<Field>"[, {by: "<Field>"}])
      Print the sum of one element field over everything the store holds, 0
      for an empty store. With {by: "<ElementField>"} print one sum and
      count per value of that field, in value order. The store must declare
      the aggregate when the bucket is created, as with (sum Amount, sum
      Amount by Currency).

  [h]oardDB [app]> db.User["ada"].Recipes.sum("Minutes")

  Elements leased by a claim and not yet acked are still in the sum. A sum
  of doubles drifts by the usual floating-point error; keep money in integer
  minor units. Pages of a by form end with a 'more:' line. Works on
  fifoStore, lifoStore, heapStore, hashStore and btreeStore. min and max are
  not kept: a btreeStore keyed by the value answers first and last for free
  (help types).

Next: help describe lists every aggregate a store keeps.
Every store and its verbs: help types.

##### help nearest
nearest — read the elements closest to a vector
===============================================

  db.<bucket>["<id>"].<storeField>.nearest({vector: <binary>, k: <n>, ...})
      Print the k elements (default 10, at most 100) of the store whose
      vector field is closest to yours, best first, each after its score.
      The vector is a binary of little-endian float32 values; metric is
      cosine (the default), dot or l2, set as metric: "l2". field: "<name>"
      names the element field that holds the vector (default Vector).

  db.User["ada"].Friends.put("grace", {since: 2024, Vector: {"$binary": {"base64": "AACAPwAAAAA=", "subType": "00"}}})
  db.User["ada"].Friends.nearest({vector: {"$binary": {"base64": "AACAPwAAAAA=", "subType": "00"}}, k: 3})
  1.000000  grace  {"Vector": {"$binary":{"base64":"AACAPwAAAAA=","subType":"00"}}, "since": 2024}

  Elements without the field are skipped and counted on the last line. Every
  element must hold a vector of your vector's length, or the call is refused
  naming the element. The embedding model is yours: hoardDB compares bytes
  and never calls a model. Works on fifoStore, lifoStore, heapStore,
  hashStore and btreeStore.

Next: help list reads every element instead.
Every store and its verbs: help types.

##### help take
take — remove and return the element under a key
================================================

  db.<bucket>["<id>"].<storeField>.take("<key>")
      Remove the element stored under a key and print it.

  [h]oardDB [app]> db.User["ada"].Friends.put("grace", {since: 2024})
  [h]oardDB [app]> db.User["ada"].Friends.take("grace")
  {"since": 2024}

  Works on hashStore and btreeStore. take on a heapStore is not available
  yet.

Next: help put.
Every store and its verbs: help types.

##### help put
put — store an element under a key
==================================

  db.<bucket>["<id>"].<storeField>.put("<key>", {…})
      Store an element under a key in a hashStore or a btreeStore, replacing
      any element that key held.

  [h]oardDB [app]> db.User["ada"].Friends.put("grace", {since: 2024})
  OK  put "grace": Friends now holds 1 element

  The document must exist first. Works on hashStore and btreeStore. For a
  fifoStore or lifoStore use push (help push). put on a blobStore is not
  available yet.

Next: db.User["ada"].Friends.take("grace") removes it again (help take).
Every store and its verbs: help types.

##### help inc
inc — add to the numbers of an element, creating it
===================================================

  db.<bucket>["<id>"].<storeField>.inc("<k>", {<p>: <n>}[, {return: "after"}])
      Add the numbers you give to the element stored under a key in a
      hashStore or a btreeStore, and create the element when the key is
      absent. Each path is a field name, or names joined by dots (up to
      three levels); each number is an int or a double.

  [h]oardDB [app]> db.User["ada"].Friends.inc("grace", {visits: 1})
  OK  inc "grace": Friends now holds 1 element

  It is one write at the owner, so two callers never lose an add, and a
  store that keeps aggregates keeps them exact. A counter may go negative. A
  retry is safe only while its request id is still remembered (by default
  the last 8192 applied records of the pair); after that it adds again, so
  read the element before you resend. A path that holds a string is refused.
  {return: "after"} prints the element as it stands after the add. Money
  belongs in integer minor units: a decimal is refused. Works on hashStore
  and btreeStore.

Next: db.User["ada"].Friends.get("grace") reads it back (help get).
Every store and its verbs: help types.

##### help claim
claim — lease elements to work on
=================================

  db.<bucket>["<id>"].<storeField>.claim([<n>])
      Lease the next element, or the next n, to one worker. An element that
      is not acked before its lease ends comes back. The first line printed
      is the lease id.

  [h]oardDB [app]> db.User["ada"].Purchases.claim(1)

  Do the work, then ack the lease id (help ack). To give the elements back
  at once, release it. Works on fifoStore and lifoStore.

Next: db.User["ada"].Purchases.ack("<lease>") finishes the lease (help ack).
Every store and its verbs: help types.

##### help ack
ack — finish a lease and remove its elements
============================================

  db.<bucket>["<id>"].<storeField>.ack("<lease>")
      The work under a lease is done: remove its elements for good.

  db.User["ada"].Purchases.ack("9f2c4e1ab7d34c1e8a0b5d6c7e8f9012")

  Works on fifoStore and lifoStore.

Next: help release gives the elements back instead.
Every store and its verbs: help types.

##### help release
release — give a lease's elements back
======================================

  db.<bucket>["<id>"].<storeField>.release("<lease>")
      Give a lease's elements back to the store now, instead of waiting for
      the lease to end.

  db.User["ada"].Purchases.release("9f2c4e1ab7d34c1e8a0b5d6c7e8f9012")

  Works on fifoStore and lifoStore.

Next: help extend keeps a lease longer.
Every store and its verbs: help types.

##### help extend
extend — keep a lease longer
============================

  db.<bucket>["<id>"].<storeField>.extend("<lease>", <ms>)
      Move a lease's deadline to the number of milliseconds from now you
      give, from 1000 to 3600000.

  db.User["ada"].Purchases.extend("9f2c4e1ab7d34c1e8a0b5d6c7e8f9012", 60000)

  Works on fifoStore and lifoStore.

Next: help ack finishes the lease.
Every store and its verbs: help types.

##### help types
Field types
===========

Scalars (one value per document):
  string  int32  int  double  decimal  number  bool  datetime  bsontimestamp
  binary  uuid  objectid  object  array  regex  null  any

Stores (every document owns its own; you pick the structure):
  fifoStore   a queue: pop takes the oldest  push pop peek list count sum
                                             nearest claim get
  lifoStore   a stack: pop takes the newest  push pop peek list count sum
                                             nearest claim get
  hashStore   elements by key                list count sum nearest take put inc
                                             get
  btreeStore  elements by key, in key order  list count sum nearest take put inc
                                             get
  heapStore   elements by priority           pop peek list count sum nearest get
  blobStore   large values and files         count

  create bucket User in app { Name string, Age int, Photos lifoStore,
      Friends hashStore, Recipes btreeStore, Books heapStore, Data blobStore }

integer, float, boolean and date are refused; the error names the word to use.
Next: help create, help push.

##### help all
hoardDB Query Language (HQL) — Every Statement
==============================================

'help <word>' shows one statement with examples. Tab completes every word.

Schema Management:
  create database <name> [{ <options> }]        Make a database.
  create bucket <name> [in <db>] { <Field> <type>, … }  Declare a bucket.
  alter bucket <name> [in <db>] { add <Field> <type>, … }  Add or drop fields.
  drop bucket <name> [in <db>]                  Drop a bucket.

Read Operations:
  show databases [limit <n>] [cursor <hex>]     The databases you can use.
  describe database <name> [limit <n>] [cursor <hex>]  Factor and buckets.
  show buckets [in <db>] [limit <n>] [cursor <hex>]  A database's buckets.
  describe bucket <name> [in <db>]              A bucket's fields.
  db.<bucket>["<id>"].get([{…}])                One document by id.
  db.<bucket>.list([{…}])                       Documents, a page at a time.
  db.<bucket>.count([{…}])                      How many documents.

Write Operations:
  db.<bucket>.insert({…})                       Add a document.
  db.<bucket>.upsert({…})                       Add, or replace the same _id_.
  db.<bucket>.update({…})                       Change some of its fields.
  db.<bucket>.delete({…})                       Remove it and its stores.

Store Fields (each document's own; help types):
  db.<bucket>["<id>"].<storeField>.<verb>([…])  push pop peek list count sum
                                                describe nearest take put inc
                                                claim ack release extend get

User Management:
  create user <name> [roles [<role>@<db>, …]]   Create a user.
  alter user <name> disable|enable|password     Block, unblock, new password.
  alter user <name> roles [<role>@<db>, …]      Replace a user's roles.
  drop user <name>                              Delete a user.
  grant role <role>@<db> to <user>              Add one grant.
  revoke role <role>@<db> from <user>           Remove one grant.
  show users                                    Users, roles and status.
  user list                                     The same as show users.
  user describe <name>                          One user's roles and status.

Cluster Management:
  join <node>@<host:port>                       Register a running node.
  promote <node>                                Make a member a voter.
  replace-node <node> --with <node>@<host:port> [--accept-loss]
                                                Reseed a node that lost data.
  nodes                                         List the members.
  status seeding [<db> [<slot>]]                Slot pulls in progress.
  status                                        Epoch, leader and lease.
  seeding retry <db> <slot>                     Restart one slot's pull.
  seeding forget <db> <slot> [anyway]           Drop a retired slot's record.

Session Commands:
  use <database>                                Work inside a database.
  show banner                                   Draw the node summary again.
  set <setting> [on|off]                        pretty, extjson or pager.
  help [<topic>]                                Quick Start, or one statement.
  exit                                          Leave; quit works too.

Not available yet:
  alter database, drop database, leave, remove-node, show placement,
  show replication

##### help create database
create database — make a database
=================================

  create database <name> [{ <options> }]

  create database app                            One copy (this node's default).
  create database app { replication_factor: 1 }  Say how many copies.

  Options: { replication_factor: <n>, storage: disk }. More than one copy
  needs that many data nodes: a factor the cluster cannot hold is refused
  before anything is written. Every database is kept on disk in this build.

##### help alter database
alter database — change its replication factor
==============================================

  alter database <name> { <options> }

  Not available yet.

##### help drop database
drop database — drop a database
===============================

  drop database <name>

  Not available yet.

##### help create bucket
create bucket — declare a bucket
================================

  create bucket <name> [in <db>] { <Field> <type>, … }

  create bucket User { Name string, Purchases fifoStore }
  create bucket Order in shop { Total double, Lines fifoStore, Notes hashStore }

  Declare every field. A field is a scalar (string, int, double, bool,
  datetime, …) or a store, and a store belongs to each document: every User
  has its own Purchases queue. A store keeps a sum or a count current when
  you add with (sum Total, count by Status) after it. Not built yet, so
  refused: index <IndexName> ( <Field> [desc] ). All the words: help types.
  Like SQL CREATE TABLE, except a field can be a store each row owns.

Next: use app, then db.User.insert({_id_: "ada", Name: "Ada"})

##### help alter bucket
alter bucket — add or drop fields
=================================

  alter bucket <name> [in <db>] { add <Field> <type>, … }

  alter bucket User { add Email string, drop Age }
  alter bucket User in app { add Notes hashStore }

  Add fields to a bucket or drop them, in one statement: { add Email string,
  drop Age }. Adding rewrites no document: every existing document reads the
  new field as unset, and a store field is empty until its first push.
  Dropping a field makes it unreadable at once and its values are deleted in
  the background; the name can be added again later as a new field. A
  field's type never changes: add a new field, copy the values, drop the old
  one. Not built yet, so refused: add index <IndexName> ( <Field> [desc], …
  ) and drop index <IndexName>.
  Like SQL ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN and DROP COLUMN, except an add costs
  nothing however many rows there are.

Next: describe bucket User, to see the fields now in force

##### help drop bucket
drop bucket — drop a bucket
===========================

  drop bucket <name> [in <db>]

  drop bucket User
  drop bucket User in app

  Drop a bucket and every document and store it holds. The bucket stops
  answering at once and the statement returns; its documents are deleted in
  the background, 'show buckets' lists it as dropping until they are gone,
  and the name is free to create again then (the new bucket is empty). There
  is no prompt and no undo: run 'describe bucket' first if you are not sure
  which bucket this is. Repeating a drop of a dropping bucket is safe, but a
  drop repeated after it finished and the name was created again drops the
  new bucket.
  Like SQL DROP TABLE.

Next: show buckets, to watch it leave the list

##### help show databases
show databases — the databases you can use
==========================================

  show databases [limit <n>] [cursor <hex>]

  show databases
  show databases limit 10

  List the databases you hold a role on with their id and replication
  factor, a page at a time; a page that is not the last prints the statement
  that fetches the next.

Next: use app

##### help describe database
describe database — factor and buckets
======================================

  describe database <name> [limit <n>] [cursor <hex>]

  describe database app
  describe database app limit 10

  Show a database's id and replication factor and a page of its buckets.

Next: describe bucket User

##### help show buckets
show buckets — a database's buckets
===================================

  show buckets [in <db>] [limit <n>] [cursor <hex>]

  show buckets in app
  show buckets
  show buckets in app limit 10

  List a database's buckets, a page at a time; a page that is not the last
  prints the statement that fetches the next.

Next: describe bucket User

##### help describe bucket
describe bucket — a bucket's fields
===================================

  describe bucket <name> [in <db>]

  describe bucket User in app
  describe bucket User

  Show a bucket's declaration: its id, state and fields in field-id order.

Next: db.User.list()

##### help get
get — read one
==============

On a bucket (its documents):
  db.<bucket>["<id>"].get([{…}])

  db.User["ada"].get()
  db.User["ada"].get({fields: ["Name"]})

On a store field (one document's elements):
  db.<bucket>["<id>"].<storeField>.get("<key>")

  db.User["ada"].Friends.get("grace")

On a fifoStore or lifoStore get takes the lease id claim printed and prints
the elements that lease still holds:
db.User["ada"].Purchases.get("9f2c4e1ab7d34c1e8a0b5d6c7e8f9012").
Works on fifoStore, lifoStore, hashStore, btreeStore and heapStore.
Next: help take removes it instead; help claim makes a lease.

##### help list
list — read many, a page at a time
==================================

On a bucket (its documents):
  db.<bucket>.list([{…}])

  db.User.list()
  db.User.list({limit: 10})
  db.User.list({filter: {Name: "Ada"}, limit: 10})
  db.User.list({fields: ["Name", "Age"], limit: 10})

On a store field (one document's elements):
  db.<bucket>["<id>"].<storeField>.list([{limit: <n>, cursor: "<hex>"}])

  db.User["ada"].Purchases.list({limit: 10})

A page that is not the last ends with a 'more:' line: the same statement
with the cursor filled in. Run it for the next page.
Works on fifoStore, lifoStore, heapStore, hashStore and btreeStore.
Next: help count.

##### help count
count — how many
================

On a bucket (its documents):
  db.<bucket>.count([{…}])

  db.User.count()
  db.User.count({filter: {Name: "Ada"}})

On a store field (one document's elements):
  db.<bucket>["<id>"].<storeField>.count([{by: "<Field>"}])

  db.User["ada"].Purchases.count()

On a bucket with 'expire at', count reads one extra key per document, and on
a store that expires its elements it walks them, because it must skip what
has expired (help expire).
Works on fifoStore, lifoStore, heapStore, hashStore, btreeStore and
blobStore.
Next: help list.

##### help insert
insert — add a document
=======================

  db.<bucket>.insert({…})

  db.User.insert({_id_: "ada", Name: "Ada"})

  Add a document; an existing _id_ is refused. A store field is written
  through its own verb, never here.

Next: db.User["ada"].get()

##### help upsert
upsert — add, or replace the same _id_
======================================

  db.<bucket>.upsert({…})

  db.User.upsert({_id_: "ada", Name: "Ada Lovelace"})

  Add a document or replace the one with the same _id_.

Next: db.User["ada"].get()

##### help update
update — change some of its fields
==================================

  db.<bucket>.update({…})

  db.User.update({_id_: "ada", $set: {Name: "Ada King"}})
  db.User.update({_id_: "ada", $set: {Name: "Ada King"}, $inc: {Age: 1}})
  db.User.update({_id_: "ada", if: {Name: "Ada"}, $set: {Name: "Ada King"}, return: "after"})
  db.User.update({_id_: "ada", $inc: {Age: 1}, return: "before"})

  Change fields of the document with this _id_, with update operators ($set,
  $inc and the rest). All of them apply together or none does. Add if:
  {Field: value} to change it only while those scalar fields still hold
  those values (null matches a field that was never set); if one differs
  nothing changes and you get the current document. Add return: "after" or
  "before" to get the document back. An upsert takes no if.

Next: db.User["ada"].get()

##### help delete
delete — remove it and its stores
=================================

  db.<bucket>.delete({…})

  db.User.delete({_id_: "ada"})
  db.User.delete({_id_: "ada", if: {Name: "Ada"}})

  Delete the document with this _id_ and the stores it owns. Add if: {Field:
  value} to delete it only while those scalar fields still hold those
  values, and return: "before" to get the document as it was.

Next: db.User.count()

##### help create user
create user — create a user
===========================

  create user <name> [roles [<role>@<db>, …]]

  create user ada roles [reader@app]
  create user ada
  create user ada roles [writer@app, reader@logs]

  Create a user. The password is asked twice with no echo, or read from
  -password-file or -password-stdin, never a flag. A role is <role>@<db>, or
  <role>@* for every database.

##### help alter user
alter user — block, unblock, new password
=========================================

Block, unblock, new password:
  alter user <name> disable|enable|password

  alter user ada disable
  alter user ada enable
  alter user ada password

  Block a user, let it in again, or change its password (asked twice with no
  echo).

Replace a user's roles:
  alter user <name> roles [<role>@<db>, …]

  alter user ada roles [writer@app, reader@logs]

  Replace a user's whole role set.

##### help alter user roles
alter user roles — replace a user's roles
=========================================

  alter user <name> roles [<role>@<db>, …]

  alter user ada roles [writer@app, reader@logs]

  Replace a user's whole role set.

##### help drop user
drop user — delete a user
=========================

  drop user <name>

  drop user ada

  Delete a user.

##### help grant role
grant role — add one grant
==========================

  grant role <role>@<db> to <user>

  grant role db-manager@app to ada
  grant role reader@* to ada

  Add one grant. Roles: root, reader, writer, db-manager, user-manager,
  cluster-manager, backup-operator, restore-operator.

